## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a cross-account, auditable backup strategy for SAP HANA on EC2 that enforces immutability and cross-account access control.

## Key Concepts

- Immutable backups with S3 Object Lock (COMPLIANCE)
- Cross-account IAM roles and trust policies
- KMS CMK grants for cross-account encryption/decryption
- AWS Backup vault policies and cross-account access
- S3 bucket versioning and access policies

## Code Example

```hcl
# Terraform snippet: immutable SAP backups with cross-account access
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "sap_backups" {
  bucket = "sap-backups-prod"
  acl    = "private"

  versioning {
    enabled = true
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_object_lock_configuration" "lock" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.sap_backups.id
  object_lock_enabled = true

  rule {
    default_retention {
      mode = "COMPLIANCE"
      days = 3650
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_backup_cross_account_role" {
  name = "sap-backup-cross-account-role"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow",
      Principal = {
        AWS = "arn:aws:iam::BACKUP_ACCOUNT_ID:root"
      },
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you rotate the CMK without service disruption?
- How would you validate backups remain immutable after policy changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical incident response design using AWS-native tooling in a SAP on AWS context, focusing on automation, speed, and auditable notifications.

## Key Concepts
- CloudWatch alarms and SAP heartbeat monitoring
- EventBridge as automation trigger
- Step Functions orchestration for multi-step recovery
- SSM Run Command for SAP status verification
- VPC security controls for subnet isolation
- SNS for stakeholder notifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Lambda skeleton for status check (conceptual)
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const ssm = new AWS.SSM();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const params = {
    InstanceIds: ['i-sap-instance'],
    DocumentName: 'AWS-RunShellScript',
    Parameters: {
      commands: ['HDB info | grep -i "overall status"']
    }
  };

  const result = await ssm.sendCommand(params).promise();
  return { status: 'checked', commandId: result.Command.CommandId };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design scalable, auditable cross-account access that bridges SAP authorizations with AWS IAM, while using JIT and MFA in a multi-region, multi-account setup. Tests integration points across IAM, SCPs, SSO, auditing, and basic IaC.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account trust and least privilege
- SCPs and IAM role mappings to SAP authorizations
- Just‑In‑Time elevation with AWS SSO and MFA
- Auditing: CloudTrail, Config, Central logging
- Minimal IaC for trust role and VPC endpoint service

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal Terraform-like snippet for cross-account trust role (pseudo)
provider "aws" { alias = "shared" }
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator_trust" {
  name = "sap-operator-cross-account"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow",
        Principal = { AWS = ["arn:aws:iam::111111111111:root"] },
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole",
        Condition = { StringEquals = { "sts:ExternalId" = "sap-access" } }
      }
    ]
  })
}

// Per‑BU session policy (inline for example)
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "sap-operator-permission"
  role = aws_iam_role.sap_operator_trust.name
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["ec2:Describe*"], Resource = "*" },
      { Effect = "Allow", Action = ["sap:*"], Resource = "*" }
    ]
  })
}

// VPC Endpoint Service placeholder
resource "aws_vpc_endpoint_service" "sap_endpoints" {
  acceptance_required = true
  network_load_balancer_arns = [aws_lb.sap_nlb.arn]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate the mapping between SAP authorizations and AWS roles at scale?
- How would you handle key rotation and revocation in this model?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of AWS patching automation, SAP patching options, health checks, rollback strategies, and CloudFormation for operational guardrails.

## Key Concepts
- AWS Systems Manager Maintenance Windows
- SAP patching options: SUM vs OS patches
- Pre/post patch validation: SAPControl, SAP background jobs, host metrics
- Rollback strategies: SAP backups, AMI snapshots
- CloudFormation for roles and patch baselines

## Code Example
```javascript
// CFN skeleton (pseudo)
{
  "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
  "Resources": {
    "SAPSSMRole": {
      "Type": "AWS::IAM::Role",
      "Properties": {
        "AssumeRolePolicyDocument": { "Version": "2012-10-17", "Statement": [ { "Effect": "Allow", "Principal": { "Service": ["ec2.amazonaws.com", "ssm.amazonaws.com"] }, "Action": "sts:AssumeRole" } ] },
        "ManagedPolicyArns": [ "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonSSMFullAccess" ]
      }
    },
    "SAPPatchBaseline": {
      "Type": "AWS::SSM::PatchBaseline",
      "Properties": {
        "Name": "SAPBaseline",
        "OperatingSystem": "AmazonLinux2"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify patch success in SAP and the OS without impacting production?
- How would you extend to multi-region DR alignment and scoped approvals?
